# General Discussion, Conclusion, Abstract for R15
# Each entry: (kind, text); kinds: P (Normal), H2, H3

DISC_SUMMARY = "Three main findings emerged. First, 4-year-olds, 5-year-olds, and adults all rejected RVC descriptions of zero-result events and accepted V1 descriptions of these events more often, although children accepted V1 descriptions less often than adults. Second, within pairs matched for object and target result, all three groups accepted V1 descriptions of zero-result events less often when the V1 named an action more strongly associated with the target result; the size of this association effect did not differ credibly between children and adults, and children's lower acceptance held for both stronger- and weaker-association V1s. Third, in their own descriptions, children and adults produced fewer RVCs and mentioned the target result less often for zero-result than for full-result events, and children produced fewer RVCs than adults mainly because they mentioned the result less often, not because they expressed it in other forms."

DISC = {
"rvc": "The larger effect of result realization on RVC than on V1 descriptions shows that children treat a result expressed by V2 differently from a result that is only associated with the action named by V1. This replicates the distinction found in 5-year-olds (J. Liu et al., 2026) and in a small group of children around 4;6 (Chen, 2017), and it extends it to a larger sample of 4-year-olds. On the probability scale, the difference between the two forms was present in all three groups. On the log-odds scale, the Form × Result Realization interaction was probably positive in 5-year-olds and adults (clearly so in 5-year-olds when all 10 pairs were included) but uncertain in 4-year-olds, and 4-year-olds accepted RVC descriptions of zero-result events somewhat more often (19%) than 5-year-olds (11%) and adults (1%). The distinction is therefore in place by 4 years of age for the events tested here, but it is less sharp at 4 than at 5, both because 4-year-olds occasionally accept RVC descriptions of unattained results and because they reject many V1 descriptions of these events. As noted in the Introduction, this conclusion concerns the selected RVCs and events, not RVC semantics or telicity in general.",
"rvc2": "Children accepted V1 descriptions of zero-result events less often than adults (55% and 58% vs. 83%), replicating the finding that children judge V1 descriptions of events without the result more strictly than adults do (Chen, 2017; J. Liu et al., 2026). Their stricter judgments did not make the two forms equivalent, however: Even 4-year-olds accepted V1 descriptions of zero-result events far more often than RVC descriptions. Children thus do not treat V1s as entailing the result in the way RVCs do; rather, a V1 description of an unattained result is acceptable to them in fewer cases than to adults.",
"rvc3": "The exception to children's rejection of RVC descriptions of zero-result events was Pair 9. In the videos of this pair, the stain on the mirror was a small spot in one corner, so the zero-result videos may not have shown clearly that the mirror had not become clean. Children's acceptance of the RVC descriptions of these videos therefore probably reflects the perceptibility of the result in these particular videos, that is, whether its absence could be seen, rather than an interpretation of the RVC as compatible with an unattained result. In the other pairs, in which the absence of the result was easy to see, children rarely accepted RVC descriptions of zero-result events.",
"assoc": "Children and adults rejected V1 descriptions of zero-result events more often when the V1 named an action strongly associated with the target result. Because the object and the target result were constant within pairs, this association effect cannot be attributed to differences between objects or results, which was not possible in previous studies in which each V1 was presented with a different object and result (Chen, 2017). Previous studies have also compared events across result types: Song and Haryu (2025), for example, compared shape-change and location-change events, assuming that they differ in endstate salience. When result types are compared, the type of result goes together with many other properties of the events. The present study instead held the object and the result constant within pairs, so that only the action varied, and measured the manipulated dimension independently, with adult ratings of result likelihood and action typicality, cross-checked against corpus frequencies of the corresponding RVCs. The effect was carried by most pairs rather than by one or two, and it was found with all 10 pairs and with each pair left out in turn. For both children and adults, then, what a V1 description conveys about a result depends on how the named action relates to that result. In adults, this is consistent with the cline in the acceptability of result cancellation across verbs (Chen, 2018) and with evidence that comprehenders' expectations about events depend on the combination of a verb with its arguments (Bicknell et al., 2010; McRae & Matsuki, 2009). In children, it shows that the variation among V1s that Chen (2017) attributed to the strength of the state-change implicature can be found when the event is held constant and only the action varies.",
"assoc_why": "Why action–result association matters is not settled by previous work. Accounts of monomorphemic verbs hold that a verb can imply a result without entailing it and that the strength of the implication varies, whether across verbs (Chen, 2018) or across fulfillment types (Kou & Hohenstein, 2022, 2024), but they do not specify what that strength depends on. The two ratings point to two possibilities. Result likelihood bears on the culmination inference: The more likely the result is to follow the action, the stronger the expectation that the event culminated, and the less acceptable a V1 description may seem when the result is absent. Action typicality bears on what counts as a good instance of the action. The actions that achieve a given result can be viewed as a goal-derived category, in which typicality reflects how well members serve the goal and how often they are encountered as members (Barsalou, 1985); because children are reluctant to extend familiar verbs beyond typical instances of the actions they name (Ma et al., 2021), a failed attempt at a typical means may make a V1 description less acceptable. The two dimensions usually go together but need not coincide (Kim & Murphy, 2011). These possibilities are offered as interpretations of the association effect, not as hypotheses the study was designed to test.",
"assoc_age": "The association effect did not differ credibly between children and adults, and children accepted V1 descriptions of zero-result events less often than adults for both stronger- and weaker-association V1s. This pattern matches the predictions and the initial analysis with the original Strong/Weak coding. The comparison between children and adults was inconclusive rather than null, however: The effect was, if anything, smaller in children on the log-odds scale and larger in children on the probability scale, because adults accepted weaker-association V1s almost always, and the intervals on both scales were wide. With this caveat, the results suggest that children are sensitive to action–result association in the same way as adults and that their stricter judgments do not arise only for V1s that name strongly associated actions. Children's lower acceptance then reflects a more general difference in how they evaluate V1 descriptions of failed attempts. Three explanations of this difference cannot be separated by the binary task: Children may find it harder than adults to set aside an inference that the result occurred, may reject a true description as inadequate because it leaves an expected result unaddressed, or may judge a failed attempt to be a poor instance of the action. Because children tend to be more tolerant than adults of underinformative descriptions in binary judgment tasks (Katsos & Bishop, 2011), the second explanation would require that a description that leaves the result unaddressed is perceived as more than underinformative when an attempt has failed. The production data, discussed below, bear on these explanations.",
"assoc_conf": "Because the stronger- and weaker-association expressions differ in both rated likelihood and rated typicality, the study cannot determine which dimension, or what the two share, underlies the association effect; the two ratings diverged only in Pair 7, which was excluded. The contrast between stronger- and weaker-association expressions also goes together with a difference in the conventionality of the corresponding RVCs. A child who often hears *chuī-miè* \"blow-extinguished\" may come to expect a candle to go out after *chuī* \"blow,\" so familiarity with the RVC offers an alternative account of the association effect. This account is not incompatible with the association account, because an action that reliably brings about a result is also likely to be described often with the corresponding RVC, but the present materials cannot separate the two. Pair 6 offers a small hint, because the RVC with the weaker-association V1, *tuī-dǎo* \"push-fallen,\" was far more frequent than *tī-dǎo* \"kick-fallen\"; children nevertheless accepted *tī* \"kick\" less often than *tuī* \"push\" in zero-result events, as the ratings predict. A single pair cannot decide between the accounts, and the frequency of *tuī-dǎo* reflects mainly abstract uses.",
"prod": "Comparing RVC use with mention of the target result separates the forms speakers used from the information they expressed. All groups mentioned the target result less often for zero-result than for full-result events, and 5-year-olds and adults also produced fewer RVCs; 4-year-olds produced few RVCs in either condition. RVC use and mention of the target result declined together, and in zero-result events responses that mentioned the result in another form, in a separate predicate or without the action, were rare. Speakers describing a failed attempt thus tended to leave the result unmentioned rather than express it in another form. When adults mentioned the result in a zero-result event, they usually denied it (e.g., *méi zá-kāi* \"did not smash open\") or presented it as the actor's goal (e.g., *shìtú sī-kāi* \"tried to tear open\"), so their RVCs in zero-result events typically appeared in negated, potential, or attempt constructions. Children rarely mentioned the result in zero-result events, and when they did, they mostly denied it, often with potential forms such as *qiāo bù kāi* \"knock-cannot-open.\" Responses affirming the target result in a zero-result event were rare in all groups (1%–4% of zero-result responses), so children did not describe an unattained result as attained.",
"prod2": "Children produced fewer RVCs than adults in both result conditions. In full-result events, children's descriptions more often named the action alone, and children did not express the result in separate predicates or without the action more often than adults. Children's lower RVC use therefore reflects less frequent mention of the target result rather than a different way of packaging it. This is consistent with evidence that children can form RVCs (Chen, 2008; Deng, 2019; Hsu et al., 2019) but shows that, in an open-ended task, they often do not express the result even when it is attained. RVC frequency alone therefore underestimates what children know about RVCs, and comparing RVC use with mention of the result is needed to interpret it. Because children responded orally and adults in writing, part of this difference may reflect response modality; adults' written descriptions were on average more than twice as long as children's oral descriptions (15 vs. 6 characters).",
"prod_instr": "The elicitation questions bear on these differences. Children were first asked *Nǐ kàn-dào le shénme ya?* \"What did you see?\" and, if they named only an object, *Nà zhège rén zài zuò shénme ya?* \"Then what is this person doing?\" The first question does not direct attention to what happened to the object, and the second, with the progressive marker *zài*, asks about the ongoing activity, which an action-only description answers fully. Adults were asked to describe in one sentence what they saw. Song and Haryu (2026) asked Mandarin-speaking children to describe what the agent did to the object and reported that children, like adults, produced RVCs for most events with a change of state and action verbs for most events without one. The low rate of RVCs in children's descriptions of full-result events in the present study therefore probably reflects in part the open prompt and the follow-up question rather than difficulty in forming RVCs. This strengthens the conclusion that children's RVC use in open-ended description depends on which information they choose to express, and it implies that comparisons of RVC frequencies across studies need to take the elicitation question into account.",
"prod3": "The two experiments also present an asymmetry at the group level. In production, children described most zero-result events with action-only responses, that is, with the kind of V1 description they rejected in about 40% of comprehension trials. If children judged V1 descriptions of failed attempts to be false because the V1 entails the result, they should avoid such descriptions in production as well. That they produced them freely suggests that their rejections in comprehension do not reflect a lexical entailment but how they evaluate a description supplied to them, for example as leaving an expected result unaddressed or as describing a poor instance of the action. This interpretation is tentative, because comprehension and production were tested in independent samples and the tasks differ in their demands. In the comprehension task, participants were told that the experimenter would say what they had seen in each video and were asked to judge whether this was right or wrong. This framing asks them to evaluate a description as a report of the event, and a report that names the action but says nothing about an expected and visibly unattained result may seem wrong to a child even if it is literally true. In the production task, by contrast, children were asked only what they had seen, which an action-only description answers. A within-participant comparison of comprehension and production would test the interpretation directly.",
"lim": "Several limitations follow from the design and from the materials. First, the yes–no task cannot distinguish a judgment that a V1 description is false from a judgment that it is true but inadequate; a graded or ternary response format (Katsos & Bishop, 2011) or a cancellation task would be needed to separate them. Second, each V1 appears with a single object and result, so the data cannot show that the same V1 conveys more or less about a result depending on the result in question; presenting the same V1 with different objects and results, such as *tī* \"kick\" with a bottle and with a door, would test this directly. Third, likelihood, typicality, and RVC conventionality covary across the retained pairs. Separating them would require pairs in which they diverge, as in the excluded Pair 7, with the two ratings collected from different raters; in the present study, the same raters always gave the likelihood ratings first. Fourth, with eight pairs and with stronger- and weaker-association V1s judged by different participants, each association effect rests on few items and small groups, and item-level estimates are descriptive; in addition, the ratings were collected and two pairs excluded after an initial analysis of the same children's data. The eight-pair results agreed with the 10-pair analyses, but the association effect needs to be replicated with a new sample of children, and the comparison of the effect between children and adults requires larger samples. Fifth, the ratings describe expressions rather than videos, and the videos did not always elicit the intended V1; in Pair 9, the stain on the mirror was small, so the zero-result videos may not have shown clearly that the mirror remained unclean. More generally, the target results differ in kind across pairs: Some involve a change of shape or integrity (a lychee coming open, paper tearing apart, a walnut cracking open, a finger bending), others a change of position or posture (a stool overturning, a bottle falling over), and others a change of surface state or light (a mirror becoming clean, a candle going out). Such differences may contribute to variation across pairs, but the comparisons of interest were made within pairs, in which both actions had the same object and the same target result. Sixth, in the production task, the follow-up question *zài zuò shénme* \"is doing what\" may have favored action-only descriptions, and responses given after this question are not distinguished from first responses in the present coding. Seventh, the production data were coded with item-specific criteria that will be checked by a second coder. ⟦人工核对与第二编码者完成后修改此句⟧ Finally, children and adults were tested in different modalities, and comprehension and production were tested in independent samples.",
}

CONCLUSION = "Mandarin-speaking 4- and 5-year-olds distinguish RVCs from their monomorphemic V1s with respect to the target result: Like adults, they reject RVC descriptions of events in which the result is not attained and accept V1 descriptions of these events more often, although less often than adults do. Their acceptance of V1 descriptions also depends on the relation between the named action and the result. When the object and the target result are held constant, children and adults alike are less willing to accept a V1 description of a failed attempt if the action is strongly associated with the result, and children's stricter judgments hold for strongly and weakly associated actions alike. In their own descriptions, children rarely express an unattained result and often leave an attained result unmentioned, so that their lower use of RVCs reflects which information they express rather than how they package it. What a monomorphemic verb conveys about a result, for children as for adults, is thus best characterized not as a fixed property of the verb but as a property of the relation between the action it names and the result in question."

ABSTRACT = "Mandarin resultative verb compounds (RVCs), such as *chuī-miè* \"blow-extinguished,\" entail the result named by the second verb, whereas their monomorphemic first verbs (V1s), such as *chuī* \"blow,\" only suggest it. This study examined how Mandarin-speaking 4- and 5-year-olds and adults interpret and describe events in which an action does or does not bring about a target result. The materials comprised pairs of actions performed on the same object with the same target result, which differed in how strongly the action was associated with the result, as verified by adult ratings of result likelihood and action typicality. In a yes–no judgment task (*N* = 104), all age groups rejected RVC descriptions of zero-result events and accepted V1 descriptions of these events more often, although children accepted V1 descriptions less often than adults. In all groups, V1 descriptions of zero-result events were accepted less often when the V1 named an action more strongly associated with the result. This association effect did not differ credibly between children and adults, and children's lower acceptance held for both stronger- and weaker-association V1s. In an elicited-production task (*N* = 104), all groups produced fewer RVCs and mentioned the result less often for zero-result events, and children produced fewer RVCs than adults mainly because they mentioned the result less often. The findings suggest that what a V1 conveys about a result depends on the relation between the named action and the result, for children as for adults."

KEYWORDS = "resultative verb compounds, Mandarin Chinese, event culmination, action–result association, verb semantics, comprehension and production"
